// The graph's DEPTH follows the session mode (`nodes-are-features-and-surfaces`).
//
// # The two node kinds, stated
//
//     a FEATURE node  = a UI the user can interact with
//     a SURFACE node  = logic, and how the AI interacts with it
//
// Both kinds existed before this and neither had a meaning written down, so they had drifted toward
// "the plan's roster" and "the crate's roster" — a description of where the data came from rather
// than of what the node is FOR. Under this reading one graph answers two questions at once: what a
// person can do here, and what an agent can do here. Everything below follows from that split being
// real, because it is what makes a single layer legible at all.
//
// # Why the mode decides how many layers there are
//
// There are three today: the project drill, then a crate sub-layer (its nouns and verbs) and a
// feature sub-layer (its streams). That is the right shape for a LOCAL model and the wrong one for
// Claude, and the reason is the same fact that sets `numCtx`: a local model is markedly more verbose
// and has to split its context far harder. A smaller view per navigation is that split expressed in
// the interface rather than only in a number — so local keeps the sub-layers, and Claude, which can
// hold the project at once, gets one layer and no navigation into a new graph.
//
// # It is a pure function on purpose
//
// A rendering rule you can only check by clicking is one nobody checks. This module is the whole
// decision and takes no store, no graph and no React — the workspace asks it a question and obeys.

/** The session modes a project can be in — the two `bsc session mode` writes. `null` is a project in
 *  neither, which is a real state: a machine with an `openai` provider resolves to no mode at all. */
export type SessionMode = "local" | "claude" | null;

/** The layers below the project drill. Named rather than counted, because "two layers" says nothing
 *  about WHICH two and a caller has to gate them individually. */
export type SubLayer = "crate" | "feature";

/**
 * Whether `layer` opens as its own graph in `mode`.
 *
 * Claude gets ONE layer: selecting a crate or a feature at the project drill does not navigate into a
 * new graph. Local keeps both, unchanged.
 *
 * A NULL MODE KEEPS THE LAYERS, and that direction is deliberate. The two failures are not
 * symmetrical: showing a sub-layer to a session that did not need it costs a click, while hiding
 * navigation from a user who had it removes the only route to a verb node. An unrecognised provider —
 * `openai`, or a machine nobody has configured — is the case that hits it, and degrading to the
 * richer view is the one that cannot strand anybody.
 */
export function opensSubLayer(mode: SessionMode, layer: SubLayer): boolean {
  void layer; // both sub-layers move together today; named so a future mode can split them.
  return mode !== "claude";
}

/**
 * How many graph layers a project has in `mode`, counting the project drill itself.
 *
 * Reported rather than derived at each call site so the answer is one number a test can pin and a
 * caller can show — "1 layer" and "3 layers" is the difference the mode makes, and it should be
 * possible to state it without reading the workspace.
 */
export function layerCount(mode: SessionMode): number {
  return mode === "claude" ? 1 : 3;
}

/** What a node kind IS, for the inspector and for anything that has to explain the graph. */
export const NODE_KIND_MEANING: Record<"feature" | "surface", string> = {
  feature: "a UI the user can interact with",
  surface: "logic, and how the AI interacts with it",
};
